-- allium: 3
-- rotation-server.allium

use "./rotation-daemon.allium" as daemon

-- Scope: Bitwarden server side of credential rotation
-- Includes:
--   Target system definition and management (id, name, rotation method automatic/manual,
--     integration kind, password policy; enable/disable)
--   Rotation configuration (linking a cipher to a target system) and its rotation
--     settings: the triggers that cause a rotation (an optional schedule and the
--     rotate-on-access-end opt-in), set per credential
--   Rotation scheduling and triggering: on a schedule (cron), on demand, and on
--     access events (an upstream grant over the credential ending)
--   Manual rotation fallback for systems that cannot be rotated automatically:
--     tracking the obligation and recording an out-of-band rotation
--   Pause/resume of a credential's rotation without losing its configuration
--   Pull-based dispatch: a due rotation becomes an available RotationJob that a
--     connected, assigned daemon claims (creating an attempt); a retry is a re-claim
--   Cipher-write authorisation: writes are bound to the daemon's own claimed
--     RotationJob (no separate nonce) -- ownership and in-flight status
--   Multi-daemon eligibility: admin-assigned daemon-to-target reachability decides
--     which daemons may claim a target's jobs; any eligible daemon claims, with
--     per-daemon recovery when one drops
--   Server-side job tracking and recovery, retry orchestration across attempts
--   Audit emission for every rotation attempt and outcome (Goal 4)
-- Excludes:
--   Daemon execution of rotations (governed by rotation-daemon.allium)
--   The daemon authentication handshake and session lifecycle (modelled in
--     rotation-daemon.allium); this spec covers only daemon registration/revocation
--   Cron-expression parsing / next-occurrence computation (deferred NextScheduledTime)
--   Collection-wide settings (applying one set of rotation settings across a whole
--     collection at once) — deferred; settings are per-credential for now
--   Failure-handling policy beyond a near-term retry — alerting, auto-disable — which
--     is §6.8 (not yet specified here; RotationFailed is the seam)
--   Bitwarden cipher/Secrets Manager internals (external systems)
--   Audit-log persistence and WORM/tamper-evidence: this spec emits the audit
--     events; durable, append-only storage is the existing Phase 1 audit log's
--     concern (mirrors how pam.allium models LeaseActivationAudited)
--   Discovery of privileged/orphaned accounts, ephemeral account provisioning,
--     dependency orchestration beyond the credential, and post-rotation scripting
--     (Phase 1 non-goals)
--   Rotation of Secrets Manager secrets (Phase 1 non-goal; see the SM seam note
--     by RotationConfig and the closing open question)
-- Dependencies:
--   daemon/Cipher and daemon/RotationAttempt are governed by rotation-daemon.allium and
--   referenced here by qualified name. The TargetSystem and its PasswordPolicy are owned
--   HERE (server-side); the daemon holds no target-system entity and receives the bits it
--   needs (id, name, kind, password policy) on each claim.

config {
    failure_retry_delay: Duration = 1.hour
    -- A rotation job's overall deadline: a job still pending or claimed past this is
    -- timed out (JobTimesOut). Bounds both an unclaimed (unroutable) job and a
    -- claimed-but-stuck daemon. Generous relative to one rotation attempt.
    job_ttl: Duration = 1.hour
    -- Per-job retry budget: rotation retries are server-orchestrated, so a failed
    -- attempt makes the job claimable again (a fresh attempt) until this many attempts
    -- have failed, after which the job fails.
    max_attempts: Integer = 5
    -- Base backoff before a failed job becomes claimable again (exponential per
    -- attempt in practice; the exact curve is an implementation detail).
    retry_base_delay: Duration = 1.second
}

-- Parties

-- The administrator is a Bitwarden user managing rotation. Their identity and
-- authentication are governed by the Bitwarden platform, outside this spec.
external entity Administrator {}

-- An upstream access-control system (PAM leasing, AAP grants, ...) that reports
-- when a grant over a managed credential ends. Modelled as an abstract integration
-- party so rotation depends on the access-event signal, not on any one leasing
-- spec. See AccessEventReporting and RotateOnAccessEnd.
actor AccessEventSource {
    identified_by: cipher.id
}

-- Value types

-- A rotation cadence. Carries a cron-style expression for exact timing (e.g. a
-- maintenance window); UI presets are a convenience that produce such an
-- expression. The next-occurrence computation is deferred (NextScheduledTime).
value Schedule {
    cron: String
}

-- The triggers that cause a credential to rotate, grouped so they can be set on a
-- credential as a unit. On-demand rotation is always available and so is not a stored
-- setting.
--   schedule: the optional schedule trigger. Absent => no scheduled rotation; the
--     credential still rotates on demand or on an access event.
--   rotate_on_access_end: opt-in to the access-event trigger (Goal 2).
value RotationSettings {
    schedule: Schedule?
    rotate_on_access_end: Boolean
}

-- What kind of password to generate for an automatic target: the constraints a new
-- password must satisfy. Server-side configuration the admin sets on the TargetSystem;
-- it is carried to the daemon on each claim (RotationClaimed), which generates a password
-- meeting it. The daemon holds a received copy of this shape (rotation-daemon.allium /
-- PasswordPolicy) since it cannot reference server types.
value PasswordPolicy {
    min_length: Integer
    max_length: Integer
    include_uppercase: Boolean
    include_lowercase: Boolean
    include_digits: Boolean
    include_symbols: Boolean
}

-- Entities

-- A target system the daemon can rotate credentials in (Entra, a SQL Server, a custom
-- script, ...). Defined and owned server-side: the admin registers it with a name and a
-- rotation method, and the server holds NO connection details -- those live in the
-- daemon's local config, keyed by this id (see rotation-daemon.allium). Status enables or
-- disables the whole target. Status literals (active|disabled) are deliberately distinct
-- from DaemonRegistration's (enrolled|revoked) so untyped trigger-param inference stays
-- unambiguous.
entity TargetSystem {
    id: String
    name: String
    -- automatic: the daemon rotates this system's credentials; needs a kind so the daemon
    --   knows which integration to run (and a password policy + session-termination
    --   capability, carried to the daemon on a claim).
    -- manual: no daemon integration -- rotations are recorded out of band (Goal 3); kind,
    --   password_policy and supports_session_termination do not apply.
    method: automatic | manual
    -- Which integration the daemon runs (Entra, MSSQL, or a custom script).
    kind: entra | mssql | custom_script when method = automatic
    password_policy: PasswordPolicy when method = automatic
    supports_session_termination: Boolean when method = automatic
    -- Enable/disable the whole target: a disabled system stops new rotations being offered
    -- for all of its credentials (jobs already claimed are unaffected).
    status: active | disabled

    transitions status {
        active -> disabled
        disabled -> active
    }
}

-- A registered rotation daemon identity. The admin registers a daemon, the server
-- issues its credential (an OAuth2 client_id + client_secret plus a local
-- encryption key, delivered out of band; only the client_id is retained here), and
-- the daemon authenticates with it (see rotation-daemon.allium / DaemonSession).
-- Revoking a registration invalidates the credential and the daemon's session.
-- Status literals are deliberately distinct from TargetSystem's (enrolled/revoked, not
-- active/disabled) so untyped trigger-param inference stays unambiguous.
entity DaemonRegistration {
    id: String
    name: String
    client_id: String
    status: enrolled | revoked

    transitions status {
        enrolled -> revoked
        terminal: revoked
    }

    -- The target systems this daemon is permitted to rotate (admin-assigned).
    assignments: DaemonTargetAssignment with daemon = this
    -- Server-side liveness: true while the daemon's connection is up (see
    -- DaemonConnection and the connectivity rules). Drives claim eligibility.
    is_connected: exists DaemonConnection{daemon: this, status: connected}
}

-- An admin-granted permission that a daemon may rotate a target system. Eligibility
-- is by reachability: a daemon may claim a rotation only for a target it is assigned.
-- Many-to-many — a target may have several assigned daemons (HA), and a daemon may
-- be assigned many targets. The assignment is the security boundary: a daemon can
-- only ever claim rotations for assigned targets.
entity DaemonTargetAssignment {
    id: String
    daemon: DaemonRegistration
    target_system: TargetSystem
}

-- The server's per-daemon view of connection liveness, used for claim eligibility
-- and per-daemon recovery. Created (disconnected) when the daemon is registered, then
-- flipped as the daemon's authenticated transport comes up or down. Distinct from
-- the daemon-side connection/session lifecycle in rotation-daemon.allium, which is
-- one daemon's own view; this is the server's per-daemon view across the fleet.
entity DaemonConnection {
    id: String
    daemon: DaemonRegistration
    status: connected | disconnected

    transitions status {
        disconnected -> connected
        connected -> disconnected
    }
}

-- A rotation configuration links a cipher to a registered target system and carries
-- its rotation settings (the triggers) and schedule state. A cipher has at most one
-- rotation configuration (which names its target system); see OneConfigPerCipher.
-- The cipher therefore uniquely identifies the configuration to rotate.
--
-- SM seam (Phase 1 non-goal): a future Secrets Manager configuration would point
-- at an SM secret rather than a cipher. The `cipher` field here and
-- daemon/RotationAttempt.cipher are the single seam to generalise; the scheduling,
-- dispatch, recovery and audit machinery below is intended to carry an SM rotation
-- unchanged. Kept cipher-specific for now to avoid speculative structure.
entity RotationConfig {
    id: String
    cipher: daemon/Cipher
    target_system: TargetSystem
    account_identity: String
    terminate_sessions: Boolean
    -- Whether this credential rotates automatically or by hand is a property of its target
    -- system (target_system.method), not the configuration: every credential on an
    -- automatic target is rotated by the daemon; every credential on a manual target is
    -- recorded out of band (Goal 3). There is no per-credential override.
    -- The rotation triggers for this credential (schedule + access-end opt-in). Set
    -- per credential; last write wins.
    settings: RotationSettings
    -- The next scheduled rotation, derived from settings.schedule. Absent when there
    -- is no schedule, so RotationDue (a temporal trigger) never fires for it.
    next_rotation_at: Timestamp?
    -- False pauses rotation without losing configuration (UX2: change-freeze).
    enabled: Boolean
    last_rotation_at: Timestamp?

    jobs: RotationJob with rotation_config = this
    active_jobs: jobs where status in {pending, claimed}
    has_active_job: active_jobs.count > 0

    -- A manual configuration that has come due: the obligation an admin must
    -- satisfy and record (see RecordManualRotation). Surfaced, never dispatched.
    awaiting_manual_rotation: target_system.method = manual and enabled
                              and next_rotation_at != null and next_rotation_at <= now
}

-- A server-side record of one rotation occurrence, from "available" (pending) through
-- a daemon claiming and executing it. The job IS the cipher-write capability and the
-- recovery/timeout unit; a daemon's attempt (daemon/RotationAttempt) is created on
-- claim and correlated back to the job via the cipher's configuration.
--   pending   : available, no daemon yet -- claimable by an eligible daemon
--   claimed   : a daemon claimed it and is making an attempt
--   succeeded : an attempt succeeded
--   failed    : every attempt failed (max_attempts reached)
--   timed_out : still unresolved past the deadline (unroutable, or a stuck daemon)
-- The single recovery path is the claimed -> pending edge: the job returns to
-- claimable and a re-claim makes the next attempt. It is taken both when an attempt
-- fails with retries left (RetryJob, after a backoff) and when the claiming daemon's
-- connection drops mid-attempt (ReleaseJob -- the claim is released so another daemon,
-- or the same one on reconnect, can re-claim). There is no separate "lost" state: a
-- daemon drop is just another reason the job becomes claimable again.
entity RotationJob {
    id: String
    rotation_config: RotationConfig
    source: scheduled | on_demand | access_end
    -- The daemon currently handling the job; null while pending (unclaimed), set on
    -- each claim, cleared on a retry or release. Recovery is scoped by it.
    claimed_by: DaemonRegistration?
    -- Attempts made so far (incremented on each claim). The job fails once
    -- max_attempts attempts have failed.
    attempt_count: Integer
    status: pending | claimed | succeeded | failed | timed_out
    created_at: Timestamp
    -- When the job (re)becomes claimable: now at creation, pushed out by a backoff
    -- after a failed attempt so retries are spaced.
    next_claimable_at: Timestamp

    -- Overall deadline; a job still pending or claimed past this is timed out.
    deadline: created_at + config.job_ttl
    -- Claimable right now: pending and its backoff has elapsed.
    is_claimable: status = pending and next_claimable_at <= now

    transitions status {
        pending -> claimed
        pending -> timed_out
        claimed -> pending
        claimed -> succeeded
        claimed -> failed
        claimed -> timed_out
        terminal: succeeded, failed, timed_out
    }
}

-- Deferred helpers (server-side)

-- The next occurrence of a schedule strictly after a given time. Encapsulates
-- cron-expression evaluation, which is an implementation concern.
deferred NextScheduledTime    -- see: server-side cron evaluation

-- Daemon registration

rule RegisterDaemon {
    when: RegisterDaemon(name, client_id)
    requires: not exists DaemonRegistration{client_id: client_id}
    ensures: DaemonRegistration.created(name: name, client_id: client_id, status: enrolled)
    @guidance
        -- The server issues the daemon's access token (client_id + client_secret and
        -- a local encryption key) out of band. The secret and encryption key are not
        -- retained server-side in recoverable form; only the client_id is kept, for
        -- identification (zero-knowledge, see rotation-daemon.allium).
}

rule InitDaemonConnection {
    when: registration: DaemonRegistration.created
    ensures: DaemonConnection.created(daemon: registration, status: disconnected)
    @guidance
        -- Each registered daemon gets one connection record, starting disconnected and
        -- flipped by the connectivity rules. Guarantees the 1:1 in OneConnectionPerDaemon.
}

rule RevokeDaemon {
    when: RevokeDaemon(registration)
    requires: registration.status = enrolled
    ensures: registration.status = revoked
    @guidance
        -- Revoking a daemon invalidates its credential. The next time the daemon
        -- interacts its session is revoked (rotation-daemon.allium /
        -- HandleSessionRevoked) and its connection closed; it cannot authenticate
        -- again until issued a new credential (see the re-issuance open question in
        -- rotation-daemon.allium).
}

-- Daemon-to-target assignment (eligibility). A daemon may claim rotations only for
-- the targets an admin has assigned it; this is the eligibility and security
-- boundary (see DaemonTargetAssignment).

rule AssignDaemonToTarget {
    when: AssignDaemonToTarget(daemon, target_system)
    requires: daemon.status = enrolled
    -- Only automatic targets are rotated by the daemon, so only they are assignable.
    requires: target_system.method = automatic
    requires: not exists DaemonTargetAssignment{daemon: daemon, target_system: target_system}
    ensures: DaemonTargetAssignment.created(daemon: daemon, target_system: target_system)
    @guidance
        -- A pending job for this target waiting for an eligible daemon becomes
        -- claimable by this daemon as soon as it is assigned (and connected) -- no
        -- re-offer needed, the job is already pending.
}

rule UnassignDaemonFromTarget {
    when: UnassignDaemonFromTarget(assignment)
    ensures: not exists assignment
    @guidance
        -- Removing an assignment stops this daemon claiming new rotations for the
        -- target. Any rotation already claimed on this daemon runs to completion;
        -- whether to also cancel it is the cancel-in-flight open question.
}

-- Daemon connection liveness (server-side). The server observes a daemon's
-- authenticated transport come up or down and flips its DaemonConnection, which
-- claim eligibility and per-daemon recovery read. The connect/disconnect edges are
-- observed here; how they map onto the daemon-side connection/session lifecycle
-- (rotation-daemon.allium) is an open question.

rule MarkDaemonConnected {
    when: DaemonConnected(registration)
    let connection = DaemonConnection{daemon: registration}
    requires: exists connection
    requires: connection.status = disconnected
    ensures: connection.status = connected
}

rule MarkDaemonDisconnected {
    when: DaemonDisconnected(registration)
    let connection = DaemonConnection{daemon: registration}
    requires: exists connection
    requires: connection.status = connected
    ensures: connection.status = disconnected
}

-- Target system registration

rule RegisterAutomaticTargetSystem {
    when: RegisterAutomaticTargetSystem(name, kind, password_policy, supports_session_termination)
    ensures: TargetSystem.created(
        name: name,
        method: automatic,
        kind: kind,
        password_policy: password_policy,
        supports_session_termination: supports_session_termination,
        status: active
    )
    @guidance
        -- The daemon rotates this system, so it carries the integration kind, the password
        -- policy new credentials must satisfy, and whether it can terminate sessions. These
        -- ride to the daemon on each claim (ClaimRotation -> RotationClaimed). Connection
        -- details are NOT held here -- the daemon resolves them locally by id.
}

rule RegisterManualTargetSystem {
    when: RegisterManualTargetSystem(name)
    ensures: TargetSystem.created(name: name, method: manual, status: active)
    @guidance
        -- A system with no daemon integration: rotations are recorded out of band (Goal 3).
        -- No kind, password policy or session-termination capability applies.
}

rule DisableTargetSystem {
    when: DisableTargetSystem(target_system)
    requires: target_system.status = active
    ensures: target_system.status = disabled
    @guidance
        -- A disabled target system stops new rotations being offered for its
        -- configurations; jobs already claimed are unaffected.
}

rule EnableTargetSystem {
    when: EnableTargetSystem(target_system)
    requires: target_system.status = disabled
    ensures: target_system.status = active
}

-- Rotation configuration

rule CreateRotationConfig {
    when: CreateRotationConfig(cipher, target_system, account_identity, terminate_sessions, settings)
    requires: target_system.status = active
    requires: not exists RotationConfig{cipher: cipher}
    let initial_next = if settings.schedule != null: NextScheduledTime(settings.schedule, now)
                       else: null
    ensures: RotationConfig.created(
        cipher: cipher,
        target_system: target_system,
        account_identity: account_identity,
        terminate_sessions: terminate_sessions,
        settings: settings,
        next_rotation_at: initial_next,
        enabled: true
    )
    @guidance
        -- Whether this config rotates automatically or by hand follows target_system.method
        -- (no per-credential mode). Incremental adoption (Goal 5): configs are created one
        -- at a time, per cipher; registering a target system and creating its first config
        -- is the whole onboarding path, with no estate-wide migration step.
}

rule UpdateRotationSettings {
    when: UpdateRotationSettings(_, rotation_config, settings)
    let next = if settings.schedule != null: NextScheduledTime(settings.schedule, now)
               else: null
    ensures: rotation_config.settings = settings
    ensures: rotation_config.next_rotation_at = next
    @guidance
        -- Editing the settings recomputes the next scheduled time immediately
        -- (recompute-on-edit). Clearing the schedule (null) leaves the credential
        -- with no scheduled rotation; on-demand and access-event triggers still work.
}

rule PauseRotation {
    when: PauseRotation(_, rotation_config)
    requires: rotation_config.enabled
    ensures: rotation_config.enabled = false
    @guidance
        -- Pause keeps all configuration and only stops new rotations being triggered
        -- (RotationDue, TriggerRotation and RotateOnAccessEnd all require enabled).
        -- For change-freeze periods (UX2). Jobs already claimed are unaffected,
        -- mirroring DisableTargetSystem.
}

rule ResumeRotation {
    when: ResumeRotation(_, rotation_config)
    requires: not rotation_config.enabled
    let next = if rotation_config.settings.schedule != null:
                   NextScheduledTime(rotation_config.settings.schedule, now)
               else: null
    ensures: rotation_config.enabled = true
    ensures: rotation_config.next_rotation_at = next
    @guidance
        -- Resuming recomputes the next scheduled time from now, so a credential
        -- paused across one or more windows does not fire a backlog on resume — the
        -- skipped windows are intentionally skipped, not caught up.
}

-- Scheduling and triggering
--
-- All rotation triggers — schedule (RotationDue), on demand (TriggerRotationNow) and
-- access events (RotateOnAccessEnd) — converge on the single OfferRotation chained
-- trigger, so OfferRotation is the one place that creates an available job (after
-- checking enabled, the target's method is automatic, the target is active, and there is
-- no active job already). The trigger carries why the rotation fired.

rule RotationDue {
    when: rotation_config: RotationConfig.next_rotation_at <= now
    requires: rotation_config.enabled
    requires: rotation_config.target_system.method = automatic
    requires: not rotation_config.has_active_job
    ensures: OfferRotation(rotation_config: rotation_config, source: scheduled)
    @guidance
        -- Fires only when next_rotation_at is set and reached (a temporal trigger on
        -- an absent field never fires, so a credential with no schedule is never due
        -- here). A manual configuration that comes due is NOT offered; its due-ness is
        -- the obligation (awaiting_manual_rotation), surfaced for an admin to clear via
        -- RecordManualRotation.
}

rule TriggerRotationNow {
    when: TriggerRotation(_, rotation_config)
    requires: rotation_config.target_system.method = automatic
    requires: rotation_config.enabled
    requires: not rotation_config.has_active_job
    ensures: OfferRotation(rotation_config: rotation_config, source: on_demand)
    @guidance
        -- On-demand rotation (Goal 2): an admin rotates a managed credential
        -- immediately, outside its schedule. Only meaningful for automatic
        -- configurations — a manual one is rotated by hand and recorded via
        -- RecordManualRotation.
}

rule RotateOnAccessEnd {
    when: AccessGrantEnded(cipher)
    let rotation_config = RotationConfig{cipher: cipher}
    requires: exists rotation_config
    requires: rotation_config.enabled
    requires: rotation_config.target_system.method = automatic
    requires: rotation_config.settings.rotate_on_access_end
    requires: not rotation_config.has_active_job
    ensures: OfferRotation(rotation_config: rotation_config, source: access_end)
    @guidance
        -- Rotation on access events (Goal 2). When an upstream access-control system
        -- signals that a grant over a managed credential has ended — a PAM lease
        -- expiring or being revoked, an AAP grant ending — the cipher's single
        -- rotation configuration (OneConfigPerCipher) is offered, if it is automatic
        -- and opted in. The cipher alone resolves the configuration, so the signal
        -- carries no account identity. The signal is intentionally abstract
        -- (AccessEventReporting): rotation depends on the event, not on any particular
        -- leasing spec, so PAM, AAP leasing or a future Secrets Manager can all emit it.
}

-- Dispatch: offer a job, a daemon claims it
--
-- Pull model. OfferRotation makes a due rotation an available (pending) job; the
-- server push-notifies eligible (connected, assigned) daemons; one of them claims it
-- via ClaimRotation, which creates the attempt the daemon executes. A retry is a
-- re-claim of the same job (see RetryJob).

rule OfferRotation {
    when: OfferRotation(rotation_config, source)
    requires: rotation_config.enabled
    requires: rotation_config.target_system.method = automatic
    requires: not rotation_config.has_active_job
    requires: rotation_config.target_system.status = active
    ensures: RotationJob.created(
        rotation_config: rotation_config,
        source: source,
        status: pending,
        attempt_count: 0,
        created_at: now,
        next_claimable_at: now
    )
    @guidance
        -- The rotation becomes an available job (pending). The server push-notifies
        -- eligible (connected, assigned) daemons so one claims it promptly rather than
        -- polling (RotationWork); with none, the job simply waits (pending) until a
        -- daemon connects or is assigned. has_active_job stops a second job being made
        -- while one is pending or claimed.
}

rule ClaimRotation {
    when: ClaimRotation(claimer, job)
    requires: job.status = pending
    requires: job.next_claimable_at <= now
    requires: claimer.status = enrolled
    requires: claimer.is_connected
    requires: exists DaemonTargetAssignment{daemon: claimer, target_system: job.rotation_config.target_system}
    let cfg = job.rotation_config
    ensures: job.status = claimed
    ensures: job.claimed_by = claimer
    ensures: job.attempt_count = job.attempt_count + 1
    ensures: RotationClaimed(
        target_system_id: cfg.target_system.id,
        target_system_name: cfg.target_system.name,
        kind: cfg.target_system.kind,
        password_policy: cfg.target_system.password_policy,
        supports_session_termination: cfg.target_system.supports_session_termination,
        cipher: cfg.cipher,
        account_identity: cfg.account_identity,
        terminate_sessions: cfg.terminate_sessions
    )
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: cfg, outcome: dispatched, source: job.source)
    @guidance
        -- An eligible daemon takes the job; the server marks it claimed and answers with
        -- the work details (RotationClaimed), from which the daemon creates the attempt it
        -- executes (rotation-daemon.allium / StartRotation). attempt_count rises per
        -- claim. First claim wins: the guard requires the job still pending, so a second
        -- concurrent claim finds it claimed and fails. The attempt is correlated back to
        -- this job via the cipher's configuration (RotationConfig{cipher} -> the config's
        -- single claimed job), so no back-reference from attempt to job is needed.
        --
        -- The claim arrives over the daemon's authenticated channel, but the server does
        -- not trust the persistent connection alone: it re-validates the claimant's OAuth
        -- access token (the client-credentials bearer token from authentication, tied to a
        -- still-active session) as it accepts the claim. This extra check at claim time
        -- means a daemon whose token has expired or whose credential was revoked cannot
        -- claim even if its connection still looks up.
}

-- Job tracking (observed from the daemon's attempt outcomes) and retry orchestration.
-- Each transition emits the matching audit event (Goal 4). The attempt is correlated
-- to its job via the cipher's configuration and that config's single claimed job.

rule MarkJobSucceeded {
    when: attempt: daemon/RotationAttempt.status transitions_to succeeded
    let cfg = RotationConfig{cipher: attempt.cipher}
    let job = RotationJob{rotation_config: cfg, status: claimed}
    requires: exists job
    let next = if cfg.settings.schedule != null: NextScheduledTime(cfg.settings.schedule, now)
               else: null
    ensures: job.status = succeeded
    ensures: cfg.last_rotation_at = now
    ensures: cfg.next_rotation_at = next
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: cfg, outcome: succeeded, source: job.source)
    @guidance
        -- The attempt succeeded: the job is done and the next scheduled time advances
        -- to the schedule's next occurrence (or null if the schedule was cleared).
}

rule RetryJob {
    when: attempt: daemon/RotationAttempt.status transitions_to failed
    let cfg = RotationConfig{cipher: attempt.cipher}
    let job = RotationJob{rotation_config: cfg, status: claimed}
    requires: exists job
    requires: job.attempt_count < config.max_attempts
    ensures: job.status = pending
    ensures: job.claimed_by = null
    ensures: job.next_claimable_at = now + config.retry_base_delay
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: cfg, outcome: attempt_failed, source: job.source, reason: attempt.failure_reason)
    @guidance
        -- The attempt failed but the job has retries left: it returns to claimable
        -- (pending) after a backoff (exponential in practice; retry_base_delay is the
        -- base), and the next eligible daemon to claim it makes a fresh attempt -- the
        -- same daemon or another assigned one, so a struggling daemon's retry can fail
        -- over for free. Each attempt is independent and starts from a fresh password.
}

rule FailJob {
    when: attempt: daemon/RotationAttempt.status transitions_to failed
    let cfg = RotationConfig{cipher: attempt.cipher}
    let job = RotationJob{rotation_config: cfg, status: claimed}
    requires: exists job
    requires: job.attempt_count >= config.max_attempts
    ensures: job.status = failed
    ensures: cfg.next_rotation_at = now + config.failure_retry_delay
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: cfg, outcome: failed, source: job.source, reason: attempt.failure_reason)
    ensures: RotationFailed(rotation_config: cfg, reason: attempt.failure_reason)
    @guidance
        -- Attempts exhausted (max_attempts). The job fails; the config is rescheduled
        -- to a near-term retry (failure_retry_delay) and RotationFailed is emitted for
        -- the §6.8 failure-handling policy (alerting / auto-disable), not specified
        -- here. The near-term reschedule keeps the credential live until §6.8 lands.
}

rule JobTimesOut {
    when: job: RotationJob.deadline <= now
    requires: job.status in {pending, claimed}
    let cfg = job.rotation_config
    ensures: job.status = timed_out
    ensures: cfg.next_rotation_at = now + config.failure_retry_delay
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: cfg, outcome: timed_out, source: job.source)
    ensures: RotationFailed(rotation_config: cfg, reason: "rotation job timed out")
    @guidance
        -- Backstop. A job still unresolved past its deadline is declared timed_out --
        -- whether it never found a daemon (pending, unroutable) or a claiming daemon
        -- went unresponsive (claimed, stuck). It stops wedging the configuration
        -- (has_active_job), the config is rescheduled, and RotationFailed is emitted. A
        -- late result from an abandoned attempt is fenced off: the job is no longer
        -- claimed, so MarkJobSucceeded and AcceptCipherUpdate refuse it.
}

-- Recovery

rule DaemonConnectionDropsReleaseJobs {
    when: connection: DaemonConnection.status transitions_to disconnected
    for job in RotationJobs where claimed_by = connection.daemon and status = claimed:
        ensures: ReleaseJob(job: job)
    @guidance
        -- Per-daemon recovery: only jobs claimed by the daemon whose connection dropped
        -- are released (RotationJob.claimed_by scopes it); other daemons' jobs are untouched.
        -- Each released job returns to claimable (ReleaseJob), so another connected
        -- assigned daemon -- or this one on reconnect -- can re-claim it.
}

rule ReleaseJob {
    when: ReleaseJob(job)
    requires: job.status = claimed
    let cfg = job.rotation_config
    ensures: job.status = pending
    ensures: job.claimed_by = null
    ensures: job.attempt_count = job.attempt_count - 1
    ensures: job.next_claimable_at = now + config.retry_base_delay
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: cfg, outcome: released, source: job.source)
    @guidance
        -- The daemon holding this job dropped before reporting an outcome, so its claim
        -- is released: the same job returns to claimable (pending) after a backoff, and
        -- any connected assigned daemon re-claims it for a fresh attempt. The claim is
        -- undone rather than counted -- attempt_count is decremented back -- so losing
        -- connectivity is not charged against the retry budget (a genuine attempt failure
        -- is, via RetryJob). This is the same claimed -> pending recovery edge as a retry;
        -- a daemon drop is simply another reason to take it. The job_ttl deadline
        -- (JobTimesOut) remains the backstop for a job that never resolves at all.
}

-- Cipher write authorisation
--
-- The daemon writes the rotated, encrypted password back to a cipher (CipherWriteback)
-- over its authenticated channel, referencing the attempt it is executing. There is no
-- separate write token: the server's own claimed RotationJob for that cipher is the
-- capability. A write is accepted only against a job claimed by this daemon; an invalid
-- reference is refused and audited.

rule AcceptCipherUpdate {
    when: SubmitCipherUpdate(writer, attempt, new_password)
    requires: attempt.status = executing
    let cfg = RotationConfig{cipher: attempt.cipher}
    let job = RotationJob{rotation_config: cfg, status: claimed}
    requires: exists job
    requires: job.claimed_by = writer
    ensures: attempt.cipher.password = new_password
    @guidance
        -- The write is a full-object PUT (no partial-content API): the daemon resends
        -- the cipher's other fields unchanged and the server replaces the record;
        -- attempt.cipher.password = new_password is the intended net change. new_password
        -- is ciphertext the daemon encrypted with the organization key; the server
        -- persists it without decrypting (zero-knowledge). The claimed job IS the
        -- capability -- ownership (job.claimed_by) and claimed (in-flight) status authorise
        -- it, with no separate nonce. A completed, timed-out, or released job (or one
        -- re-claimed by another daemon) is no longer claimed by this writer, so a stale
        -- write is refused (RejectCipherUpdate).
}

rule RejectCipherUpdate {
    when: SubmitCipherUpdate(writer, attempt, new_password)
    let cfg = RotationConfig{cipher: attempt.cipher}
    let job = RotationJob{rotation_config: cfg, status: claimed}
    requires: not exists job or job.claimed_by != writer
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: cfg, outcome: write_rejected)
    @guidance
        -- Refused: there is no claimed job for this cipher (it completed, timed out, or
        -- its claim was released by a daemon drop), or the claimed job belongs to a
        -- different daemon. The daemon
        -- surfaces this as a failed attempt (rotation-daemon.allium / MarkAttemptFailed);
        -- if the job has retries left it becomes claimable again for a fresh attempt.
}

-- Manual rotation fallback (Goal 3)

rule RecordManualRotation {
    when: RecordManualRotation(actor, rotation_config)
    requires: rotation_config.target_system.method = manual
    let next = if rotation_config.settings.schedule != null:
                   NextScheduledTime(rotation_config.settings.schedule, now)
               else: null
    ensures: rotation_config.last_rotation_at = now
    ensures: rotation_config.next_rotation_at = next
    ensures: RotationAudited(rotation_config: rotation_config, outcome: manual_recorded, actor: actor)
    @guidance
        -- For a manually-managed configuration the daemon never rotates; instead
        -- the obligation surfaces (awaiting_manual_rotation) and an admin records
        -- that they rotated the credential out of band. Recording resets the
        -- schedule (to the schedule's next occurrence, or null) and writes the audit
        -- trail, exactly as a successful automatic rotation would — an honest record
        -- that the obligation was met without pretending the system can self-rotate.
}

-- Audit
--
-- RotationAudited is emitted on every rotation attempt and outcome — a claim (the
-- attempt, dispatched), its success (succeeded), a failed attempt that will be retried
-- (attempt_failed), final failure (failed), a released claim after a daemon drop
-- (released), timeout (timed_out), a refused cipher write (write_rejected), and a
-- recorded manual rotation (manual_recorded)
-- (Goal 4). It is a pure sink: no rule chains from it. Durable, append-only,
-- tamper-evident (WORM) storage is the existing Phase 1 audit log's job and is out
-- of scope here; this spec models the event, not its persistence (mirroring
-- pam.allium's LeaseActivationAudited). `source` carries why the rotation fired
-- (scheduled / on_demand / access_end) on every job-derived outcome; `reason` is the
-- daemon's failure_reason on an attempt_failed or failed outcome, or a server-side
-- reason on a timed_out outcome; `actor` is the recording admin on a manual_recorded
-- outcome. Each is absent where it does not apply.

-- Invariants

invariant OneConfigPerCipher {
    -- A cipher is under at most one rotation configuration. The configuration names
    -- the target system, so the cipher uniquely identifies what to rotate and where
    -- (relied on by RotateOnAccessEnd, the attempt-to-job correlation, and the
    -- RotationConfig{cipher} lookup).
    for a in RotationConfigs:
        for b in RotationConfigs:
            a != b implies a.cipher != b.cipher
}

invariant AtMostOneActiveJobPerConfig {
    -- At most one pending-or-claimed job per configuration. This makes the
    -- attempt-to-job correlation (the config's single claimed job) unambiguous and
    -- prevents concurrent rotations of one credential.
    for rotation_config in RotationConfigs:
        rotation_config.active_jobs.count <= 1
}

invariant ManualConfigNeverJobbed {
    -- A configuration on a manual target is never offered to the daemon; its rotations
    -- are recorded out of band. (A target's method is fixed at creation in Phase 1 — see
    -- the method-switching open question.)
    for c in RotationConfigs where target_system.method = manual:
        c.jobs.count = 0
}

invariant OneAssignmentPerDaemonTarget {
    -- At most one assignment per (daemon, target) pair.
    for a in DaemonTargetAssignments:
        for b in DaemonTargetAssignments:
            a != b implies (a.daemon != b.daemon or a.target_system != b.target_system)
}

invariant OneConnectionPerDaemon {
    -- Each daemon has a single connection record (created with the registration).
    for a in DaemonConnections:
        for b in DaemonConnections:
            a != b implies a.daemon != b.daemon
}

-- Surfaces

surface RotationAdministration {
    facing _: Administrator

    provides:
        RegisterDaemon(name, client_id)
        RegisterAutomaticTargetSystem(name, kind, password_policy, supports_session_termination)
        RegisterManualTargetSystem(name)
        CreateRotationConfig(cipher, target_system, account_identity, terminate_sessions, settings)
}

surface DaemonRegistrationView {
    facing _: Administrator
    context registration: DaemonRegistration

    exposes:
        registration.name
        registration.client_id
        registration.status
        -- Live eligibility state: connection liveness and the targets this daemon may rotate.
        registration.is_connected
        registration.assignments

    provides:
        RevokeDaemon(registration)
            when registration.status = enrolled
        -- Assign / unassign the targets this daemon may rotate (its eligibility).
        AssignDaemonToTarget(registration, target_system)
            when registration.status = enrolled
        UnassignDaemonFromTarget(assignment)
}

surface TargetSystemView {
    facing _: Administrator
    context target_system: TargetSystem

    exposes:
        target_system.name
        target_system.method
        -- The integration kind (present only for an automatic target).
        target_system.kind
        target_system.status
    provides:
        DisableTargetSystem(target_system)
            when target_system.status = active
        EnableTargetSystem(target_system)
            when target_system.status = disabled
}

surface RotationConfigView {
    -- A single rotation configuration: its settings (schedule + access-end opt-in), its
    -- target's rotation method, the standing obligation for a manual target, and the admin
    -- actions.
    facing admin: Administrator
    context rotation_config: RotationConfig

    exposes:
        rotation_config.cipher
        rotation_config.target_system.name
        rotation_config.target_system.method
        rotation_config.account_identity
        rotation_config.enabled
        -- The triggers (schedule + rotate_on_access_end) in one value.
        rotation_config.settings
        rotation_config.last_rotation_at
        rotation_config.next_rotation_at
        rotation_config.has_active_job
        -- The flag that drives the manual-obligation prompt (Goal 3).
        rotation_config.awaiting_manual_rotation

    provides:
        -- On-demand rotation for a credential on an automatic target (Goal 2).
        TriggerRotation(admin, rotation_config)
            when rotation_config.target_system.method = automatic
             and rotation_config.enabled
             and not rotation_config.has_active_job
        -- Record an out-of-band rotation for a credential on a manual target (Goal 3).
        RecordManualRotation(admin, rotation_config)
            when rotation_config.target_system.method = manual
        -- Edit the schedule / triggers for this one credential (recompute-on-edit).
        UpdateRotationSettings(admin, rotation_config, settings)
        -- Pause / resume without losing configuration (UX2).
        PauseRotation(admin, rotation_config)
            when rotation_config.enabled
        ResumeRotation(admin, rotation_config)
            when not rotation_config.enabled
}

surface RotationScheduleListView {
    -- List of managed credentials and their schedules, so an operator can see at a
    -- glance what is due to rotate next — what is about to change tonight (UX1).
    facing _: Administrator

    exposes:
        for c in RotationConfigs:
            c.cipher
            c.target_system.name
            c.target_system.method
            c.enabled
            c.settings
            c.last_rotation_at
            c.next_rotation_at
            c.has_active_job
            c.awaiting_manual_rotation
}

surface AccessEventReporting {
    -- Inbound integration boundary: an upstream access-control system reports that
    -- a grant over a managed credential has ended. Abstract by design — PAM, AAP
    -- leasing or (later) Secrets Manager may all be the source.
    facing _: AccessEventSource
    context cipher: daemon/Cipher

    provides:
        AccessGrantEnded(cipher)
}

surface RotationWork {
    -- The daemon's pull boundary: it claims an available job for a target it is
    -- assigned. Complemented by a server push-notification (below) so daemons pull
    -- promptly rather than poll.
    facing claimer: DaemonRegistration

    provides:
        ClaimRotation(claimer, job)
            when job.is_claimable

    @guarantee EligibleClaimsOnly
        -- A claim is accepted only for a pending, still-claimable job whose target the
        -- daemon is enrolled, connected and assigned to rotate (ClaimRotation enforces
        -- all of these). The assignment is the eligibility/security boundary. The claim is
        -- also re-authenticated: the server validates the daemon's OAuth access token on
        -- the request (defence in depth beyond the persistent connection), so an expired
        -- or revoked credential cannot claim.

    @guidance
        -- Pull with a push nudge: the server notifies eligible (connected, assigned)
        -- daemons when a job becomes claimable so they claim promptly rather than
        -- polling. First claim wins; round-robin / load-balancing emerges from
        -- whichever eligible daemon claims first, and a busy daemon simply does not claim.
}

surface CipherWriteback {
    -- The daemon writes a rotated password back to a cipher over its authenticated
    -- channel, referencing the attempt it is executing. Today's API has no partial-content
    -- update, so the daemon GETs the cipher, swaps in the newly-encrypted password
    -- (other fields ride along as opaque ciphertext) and re-PUTs the whole object. There
    -- is no separate write token: the server authorises the write against its own
    -- claimed RotationJob for that cipher (see AcceptCipherUpdate).
    facing writer: DaemonRegistration
    context attempt: daemon/RotationAttempt where status = executing

    provides:
        SubmitCipherUpdate(writer, attempt, new_password)

    @guarantee JobBoundWrite
        -- A cipher write is accepted only against a RotationJob claimed by this daemon
        -- (job.claimed_by = writer) for the attempt's cipher. The target is the attempt's
        -- cipher, so the daemon cannot redirect the write elsewhere. new_password is
        -- ciphertext encrypted with the organization key; the server stores it without
        -- decrypting (zero-knowledge). Because the write is a full-object PUT (no
        -- partial-content API), it replaces the WHOLE cipher; only the password is meant
        -- to change, and field-level write integrity is convention, not API-enforced. A
        -- dedicated partial-update endpoint and/or a separate bearer write-token
        -- (defence in depth against an ownership-check bug) would tighten this; both
        -- deferred.

    @guarantee ApiScopedReads
        -- Defence in depth for the organization key the daemon holds. The daemon's
        -- credential carries NO general cipher-read scope: the server will not
        -- enumerate ciphers for it nor serve ciphertext for any cipher except the one
        -- it is actively rotating (a claimed job). Rotation reads (GETs) only that one
        -- cipher, to re-PUT it. The organization key thus stays the theoretical crypto
        -- blast radius, but cannot be turned into broad vault disclosure without ALSO
        -- breaching this server-side API authorization.
}

surface DaemonConnectivity {
    -- The server observes a daemon's authenticated transport come up or down and
    -- flips its DaemonConnection, which claim eligibility and per-daemon recovery read.
    -- The daemon-side handshake/session lifecycle is in rotation-daemon.allium; how its
    -- transitions raise these edges is an open question.
    facing _: DaemonRegistration
    context registration: DaemonRegistration

    provides:
        DaemonConnected(registration)
        DaemonDisconnected(registration)
}

-- Open questions

-- Resolved: a daemon is registered server-side (DaemonRegistration) and authenticates
-- with a provisioned access token via an OAuth2 client-credentials grant; the session
-- lifecycle (refresh, revoke) is modelled in rotation-daemon.allium.
-- Resolved: dispatch is pull-based. A due rotation becomes an available RotationJob
-- (OfferRotation); an eligible (connected, assigned) daemon claims it (ClaimRotation),
-- which creates the attempt it executes. A retry is a re-claim: a failed attempt with
-- budget left returns the job to pending after a backoff (RetryJob). Eligibility is by
-- admin-assigned reachability (DaemonTargetAssignment); recovery is per-daemon (a
-- dropped daemon's claimed jobs are released back to claimable for re-claim).
-- Resolved: task creation is unambiguously server-side -- the server creates the
-- RotationAttempt when a daemon claims a job (ClaimRotation). The daemon no longer
-- creates tasks.
open question "Failure handling (§6.8): FailJob and JobTimesOut reschedule to a near-term retry and emit RotationFailed as the seam. §6.8 should define the real policy — alerting and auto-disable after a threshold — and take ownership of next_rotation_at on terminal failure."
open question "Collection-wide settings (deferred): applying one set of rotation settings across every managed credential in a collection at once. Deferred to keep Phase 1 per-credential -- the per-credential UpdateRotationSettings path stands in. Revisit with the collection-membership resolution it needs."
open question "Non-cron cadences: Schedule carries a cron expression for exact timing. Do interval-style presets (e.g. 'every 90 days') need a separate representation, or are all supported presets expressible as cron?"
open question "Rotation throttling: many credentials on one target coming due together could hammer that target or connector. Not modelled here (no fleet-scale deployment assumed); if needed, throttle in the daemon/connector rather than the server scheduler."
open question "Unroutable-job escalation: a job that stays pending because no eligible daemon is connected/assigned currently waits until claimed or until JobTimesOut. Past a threshold it should escalate to an operator; delivery shares the unspecified alerting seam with manual-obligation escalation and §6.8 failure handling."
open question "Daemon connectivity edges: MarkDaemonConnected/Disconnected react to server-observed DaemonConnected/DaemonDisconnected stimuli. Reconcile which daemon-side transitions (rotation-daemon.allium ServerConnection / DaemonSession) raise each edge — in particular whether a session expiry that is not a transport drop counts as disconnected for claim eligibility."
open question "Should disabling a target system, pausing a configuration, applying new settings, or unassigning a daemon cancel a claimed job, or only stop new ones being offered? (Today all only stop new ones.)"
open question "Manual obligation escalation: beyond surfacing awaiting_manual_rotation, should a manual configuration whose obligation stays unmet past a threshold escalate — notify, flag severity, or raise an alert?"
open question "Access-event emitter binding: which upstream systems wire into AccessGrantEnded (PAM leases, AAP grants, ...)? The signal-shape question is resolved — it carries only the cipher, which uniquely resolves the configuration to rotate (OneConfigPerCipher), so no account_identity is needed — but the concrete set of emitters and how they register against AccessEventReporting is still open."
open question "On-demand and access-triggered jobs record a source but not the triggering admin (only manual_recorded carries an actor). Should the audit attribute the on-demand actor end to end?"
open question "Method switching: can an admin move a target system between automatic and manual after creation (and supply/clear its kind, password policy and session-termination capability), and what becomes of its configurations' schedules and any active jobs? (ManualConfigNeverJobbed assumes a target's method is fixed at creation.)"
open question "Secrets Manager support (Phase 1 non-goal): a future configuration would point at an SM secret rather than a daemon/Cipher. Confirm the offer, claim, recovery, scheduling and audit machinery here carries an SM rotation unchanged, and decide whether to generalise the cipher seam or add a parallel SM configuration."
